1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,353
This is a small workbench for comparing the shapes of folding knives.

2
00:00:06,173 --> 00:00:13,966
On the right, about a hundred knives, rebuilt from openly published photographs and specifications.

3
00:00:14,786 --> 00:00:23,743
Each photograph has been processed: outline traced, blade and handle separated, pivot located, true scale restored.

4
00:00:24,563 --> 00:00:28,529
The tools on the left all rest on that work.

5
00:00:29,349 --> 00:00:36,059
The aim is simple: to discover, learn and try things out, on knives I own, or simply like.

6
00:00:36,879 --> 00:00:40,696
You may notice a certain fondness for Spyderco.

7
00:00:41,516 --> 00:00:44,402
Let's pick three knives.

8
00:00:45,222 --> 00:00:50,463
They can be stacked one below the other, all at the same scale,

9
00:00:51,283 --> 00:00:54,128
set side by side,

10
00:00:54,948 --> 00:00:57,945
or laid on top of each other.

11
00:00:58,765 --> 00:01:04,042
Centred on the pivot, the differences in blade and handle stand out.

12
00:01:04,862 --> 00:01:09,399
Each knife can be levelled on its pivot to tip axis,

13
00:01:10,219 --> 00:01:14,082
and the whole set turned together, about the pivot.

14
00:01:14,902 --> 00:01:20,967
After a quick screen calibration, every knife appears at its real size.

15
00:01:21,787 --> 00:01:28,750
So play with your favourite knives, compare them, and imagine how each one would behave on a cutting board.

16
00:01:29,570 --> 00:01:32,850
Suggestions are most welcome.

